{{smaller block|Her unadorned golden tresses . . . waved,<br>As the vine curls her ''tendrils.'' {{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 307.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Leaf-tendril''', a tendril consisting of a modified leaf or part of a leaf—in the latter case appearing to be borne on the leaf, as in the pea.}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Climbing as a tendril, or as by a tendril.

{{smaller block|The curling growth<br>Of ''tendril'' hops, that flaunt upon their poles. {{right|''Dyer'', Fleece, i.}}}}

'''tendril-climber''' (ten′dril-klī″mėr), ''n.'' In ''bot.'' See ''climber''<sup>1</sup>, 2.

'''tendriled, tendrilled''' (ten′drild), ''a.'' [< ''tendril'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Having tendrils; provided with tendrils.

{{smaller block|The delicate-''tendrilled'' plant must have something to cling to. {{right|''George Eliot'', Mr. Gilfil, xx.}}}}

'''tendron†''' (ten′drọn), ''n.'' [ME., < OF. ''tendron'', a shoot, tendril, also a tender person, F. ''tendron'', a shoot, a girl, gristle, < ''tendre'', tender, delicate: see ''tender''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''tendril.''] A stalk or shoot.

{{smaller block|The ''tendron'' and the leves [of a pear-tree] of thou folde. {{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 88.}}}}

'''tendry†''' (ten′dri), ''n.'' [< ''tender''<sup>2</sup> + ''-y''<sup>3</sup>.] Offer; proposal; tender. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|This confession, though imperfect, was offered: . . . the like was done also in the ''tendry'' of their larger catechism. ''Heylin'', Hist. Presbyterians, p. 473. (''Latham.'')}}

'''tendsome''' (tend′sum), ''a.'' [Also ''tensome''; < ''tend''<sup>2</sup> + ''-some.''] Requiring much attendance: as, a ''tendsome'' child. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

