'''tenebrific''' (ten-ẹ̄-brif′ik), ''a.'' [< L. ''tenebræ'', darkness, + ''facere'', make.] Producing darkness. {{smaller|According to an old fancy, night succeeds to day through the influence of tenebrific stars.}}

{{smaller block|The chief mystics in Germany, it would appear, are the transcendental philosophers, Kant, Fichte, and Schelling! With these is the chosen seat of mysticism; these are its "''tenebrific'' constellations," from which it doth "ray out darkness" over the earth.
{{right|''Carlyle'', State of German Lit.}}}}

{{smaller block|Now begins
The ''tenebrific'' passage of the tale.
{{right|''Browning'', Ring and Book, I. 123.}}}}

'''tenebrificous†''' (ten-ẹ̄-brif′i-kus), ''a.'' [< ''tenebrific'' + ''-ous''.] Tenebrific.

{{smaller block|I could mention several authors who are ''tenebrificous'' stars of the first magnitude. ''Addison'', Spectator, No. 582.}}

